    "['tunnel protection ipsec profile [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set security ipsec vpn <profile-name> bind-interface st0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `tunnel protection ipsec profile <profile-name>` command:\\n\\n##\\nset security ipsec vpn <profile-name> bind-interface st0.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper typically uses `st0` interfaces for IPsec tunnels rather than tunnel interfaces\\n2. The exact Juniper implementation may vary depending on the specific IPsec configuration and profile setup\\n3. You'd need to ensure the IPsec profile is properly configured under `security ipsec` hierarchy in JunOS\\n\\nIn Juniper, the IPsec protection is generally applied to the interface (like st0) rather than directly to a GRE tunnel as in Cisco. You might need additional configuration to combine GRE and IPsec on Juniper devices.\", 'trans_template': 'set security ipsec vpn <profile-name> bind-interface st0.0'}]]": 1,
